\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Why Does Gradient Flow Prevent Degradation?}
\label{sec:why_gradient}

The dominant pattern is not that gradient flow \emph{improves} CAR above Vanilla, but that its \emph{absence} degrades CAR below Vanilla. Three candidate mechanisms can explain this damage-prevention effect; all predict that gradient flow is necessary and label content is secondary.
(1)~\emph{Anti-overfitting regularization}: auxiliary gradients prevent the encoder from overfitting to action prediction, preserving feature diversity consistent with multi-task regularization theory~\cite{caruana1997multitask, lippl2024implicit}.
(2)~\emph{Optimization landscape smoothing}: the auxiliary loss modifies loss surface curvature, steering LoRA updates away from sharp minima that generalize poorly under distributional shift~\cite{foret2021sam}.
(3)~\emph{Gradient diversity}: auxiliary gradients provide an independent update direction that maintains the effective rank of weight updates, breaking correlations in the single-task trajectory.
Distinguishing these hypotheses requires gradient-level diagnostics (cosine similarity, norm tracking) and loss landscape analysis (Hessian spectra); we leave this to future work.

\subsection{The Detach Degradation: BatchNorm Artifact or Gradient Flow Effect?}
\label{sec:why_detach}

Detach OCC falls 3.4pp below Vanilla despite identical architecture and forward computation to Full OCC. The auxiliary head's BatchNorm2d layers update running statistics at every forward pass regardless of gradient routing; with \texttt{stop\_gradient}, the encoder bears this statistical perturbation without compensating auxiliary gradients. This forward-pass interference makes Detach worse than Vanilla: the encoder absorbs the cost of the auxiliary pathway without receiving any training benefit. The TSR instability under Detach ($\sigma = 0.163$ vs.\ $< 0.050$ for all other conditions) confirms that gradient detachment destabilizes optimization broadly.

A discriminating test replaces BatchNorm2d with LayerNorm in the auxiliary head while keeping gradients detached. \textit{[Detach+LN results pending (plan\_027, 3 seeds); outcome determines whether the 3.4pp deficit is a normalization artifact or a genuine gradient-flow effect. See Section~\ref{app:extended} for the three scenario analyses.]}

\subsection{Implications and Limitations}
\label{sec:implications}

\textbf{Practical guideline.} If an auxiliary prediction head is attached to the vision encoder during VLA fine-tuning, gradients \emph{must} flow through the encoder. Blocking gradient flow degrades both collision avoidance ($-$3.4pp) and task execution stability. With gradient flow maintained, no ground-truth spatial labels are required (Random OCC matches Full OCC), and the auxiliary head is discarded after training at zero inference cost. This training-time intervention complements runtime safety methods such as CBF-based filtering~\cite{hu2025vlsa, raja2024ogmcbf}, which provide per-step guarantees but add inference cost.

\textbf{Limitations.} All experiments use a single architecture (OpenVLA-OFT, LoRA rank 32) on a single benchmark (SafeLIBERO Level~I, 10 tasks, static obstacles). The effect size among gradient-flow conditions is modest (0.0--1.7pp, within cross-seed variance); the robust finding is the Detach degradation ($-$3.4pp). All auxiliary conditions exhibit $2.6$--$3.1\times$ higher CAR variance than Vanilla, suggesting that auxiliary gradient flow introduces systematic sensitivity to initialization regardless of label content.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{VLA Safety and Spatial Representations}

VLA models achieve high task success but remain prone to collisions~\cite{li2026vlasafety}. Two strategies address this gap. Runtime constraint methods add safety layers at inference: AEGIS~\cite{hu2025vlsa} filters actions via a CBF constructed from vision-language obstacle detection, improving CAR by 59pp on SafeLIBERO; CoFreeVLA~\cite{zhai2026cofreevla} trains a collision risk estimator for dual-arm settings; and OGM-CBF~\cite{raja2024ogmcbf} builds CBFs from occupancy grid maps. Training-time methods embed safety into the policy: SafeVLA~\cite{zhang2025safevla} applies constrained RL to reduce safety violations by 84\%. Both strategies treat safety as a behavioral constraint problem without investigating whether the VLA's internal spatial representations contribute to collision avoidance.

Separately, a growing body of work injects 3D structure into VLA vision encoders to improve task success. Depth-based methods fuse monocular predictions with visual features~\cite{yuan2025depthvla, li2025qdepthvla, rao2026augvla3d, bhat20253dcavla}, building on foundation models like Depth Anything~\cite{yang2024depthanything}. Alignment-based approaches regularize internal representations: Spatial Forcing~\cite{li2026spatialforcing} aligns VLA embeddings with pretrained 3D features (ICLR 2026); SpatialVLA~\cite{wu2025spatialvla} introduces egocentric 3D position encoding (RSS 2025). Point cloud and geometric methods encode explicit 3D structure~\cite{li2025pointvla, sun2025geovla, wu2026stvla, chen2026gstvla}. In autonomous driving, OccVLA~\cite{liu2025occvla} uses occupancy prediction as auxiliary VLA supervision, drawing on BEV representations~\cite{li2022bevformer, sima2023occnet, tian2023occ3d}. These spatial methods consistently improve task success but none evaluates collision avoidance. Our work bridges this gap: we study whether auxiliary spatial supervision affects safety, and design a factorial ablation to isolate the operative mechanism.

\subsection{Auxiliary Tasks and Multi-Task Gradient Dynamics}

Multi-task learning improves generalization by training shared representations on related objectives~\cite{caruana1997multitask, ruder2017overview}. Lippl and Lindsey~\cite{lippl2024implicit} show that multi-task training induces implicit $\ell_{1,2}$ regularization in overparameterized networks, while single-task fine-tuning exhibits nested feature selection that discards unused pretrained features. A parallel line of work addresses gradient conflicts in multi-task optimization through gradient manipulation~\cite{chen2018gradnorm, yu2020pcgrad, liu2021cagrad, navon2022nashmtl}. These methods share a common premise: task gradients may conflict, and the optimizer must resolve the conflict. Our preliminary gradient analysis shows that action and auxiliary gradients are approximately orthogonal, suggesting the auxiliary task provides an independent gradient direction rather than a conflicting one---more consistent with continual learning perspectives on preserving pretrained features~\cite{kirkpatrick2017ewc} than with gradient conflict resolution.

For VLA fine-tuning with LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora}, the interaction between auxiliary losses and adapter updates has not been studied. Among spatial VLA methods, Spatial Forcing~\cite{li2026spatialforcing} uses an auxiliary depth-alignment loss and reports improved convergence, but does not separate the regularization effect of gradient flow from the informational content of the spatial signal. Our factorial design isolates these factors.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

We fine-tune OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} with the OFT recipe~\cite{kim2025oft}, which concatenates DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024dinov2} (1024-d) and SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} (1152-d) patch features into a 2176-dimensional representation per spatial token. We apply LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} (rank 32, $\alpha{=}16$) and augment the action prediction loss with an auxiliary spatial prediction head:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{total} = \mathcal{L}_\text{action} + \lambda(t) \cdot \mathcal{L}_\text{aux}(h(\phi_v(o_t)), s_t^*),
\quad \lambda(t) = 0.01 \cdot \exp(-t / 50000)
\label{eq:total_loss}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda(t)$ decays exponentially so that spatial gradients shape early representations without dominating late-stage action refinement. The auxiliary head is discarded at inference.

\textbf{BEV occupancy head.} The head (1.2M parameters) maps the first 256 patch tokens to a $64{\times}64$ binary occupancy grid: a linear projection ($2176{\to}256$) reshaped to $16{\times}16$, two transposed convolutions ($256{\to}128{\to}64$, kernel 4, stride 2) with BatchNorm and GELU, and a $1{\times}1$ convolution with sigmoid. Ground-truth grids are rendered from MuJoCo state replay. Training uses focal loss~\cite{lin2017focal} ($\alpha{=}0.25$, $\gamma{=}2.0$).

\textbf{Monocular depth head.} Same architecture with ReLU output and smooth-L1 loss against egocentric depth maps rendered at $64{\times}64$ from MuJoCo. This head provides an egocentric spatial reference frame, complementing the allocentric BEV occupancy.

\subsection{Ablation Design}
\label{sec:ablation}

We evaluate six conditions that systematically vary gradient flow, label content, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Vanilla}: action loss only (baseline).
\item \textbf{Full OCC}: BEV occupancy with ground-truth labels; gradients flow through both encoders.
\item \textbf{Random OCC}: random binary labels with matched gradient flow; isolates gradient regularization from spatial content.
\item \textbf{Detach OCC}: ground-truth labels with encoder gradients blocked (\texttt{stop\_gradient}); tests whether gradient flow is necessary.
\item \textbf{Depth}: egocentric depth maps with gradient flow; compares allocentric vs.\ egocentric spatial frames.
\item \textbf{Cutoff}: occupancy supervision for the first 35K of 50K steps; tests temporal requirements of gradient flow.
\end{itemize}

Table~\ref{tab:ablation_design} summarizes what each pairwise comparison isolates. The chain Vanilla $\to$ Detach $\to$ Random OCC $\to$ Full OCC $\to$ Depth $\to$ Cutoff progressively disentangles auxiliary regularization, spatial content, reference frame, and temporal extent.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Ablation design: each comparison isolates one factor.}
\label{tab:ablation_design}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Comparison & Isolated Factor \\
\midrule
Full OCC vs.\ Vanilla & Auxiliary spatial supervision \\
Random vs.\ Full OCC & Spatial content vs.\ gradient flow \\
Detach vs.\ Vanilla & Necessity of encoder gradient flow \\
Depth vs.\ Full OCC & Egocentric vs.\ allocentric frame \\
Cutoff vs.\ Full OCC & Temporal extent of gradient flow \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Evaluation}
\label{sec:evaluation}

We evaluate on SafeLIBERO Level~I~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, which places obstacles in close proximity to targets across 10 tabletop manipulation tasks drawn from LIBERO-Spatial~\cite{liu2023libero}. We report \textbf{Task Success Rate (TSR)} and \textbf{Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR)}, where a collision is registered when the L1 displacement of the obstacle's \texttt{body\_xpos} exceeds $10^{-3}$. Each condition uses 3+ seeds (Depth uses 4) with 50 rollouts per task per seed. Bootstrap 95\% CIs are computed via 10{,}000 hierarchical resamples. Training uses a batch size of 8, constant learning rate $5 \times 10^{-4}$, and runs for 50K steps on NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (98\,GB); each run takes $\sim$12.9 hours. Full training details are in Appendix~\ref{app:training}.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} summarizes TSR and CAR across conditions (50 rollouts $\times$ 10 tasks per seed; bootstrap 95\% CIs via 10{,}000 hierarchical resamples).

\textbf{Gradient flow prevents degradation; label content is secondary.} All gradient-flow conditions achieve CAR within 1.7\,pp of Vanilla (0.780): Full OCC 0.797, Random OCC 0.789, Cutoff$^\dagger$ 0.783, Depth 0.780. None of these differences is statistically significant (bootstrap CIs include zero). Detach OCC (0.746), the only condition blocking encoder gradients, falls 3.4\,pp below Vanilla, with CIs excluding zero for all pairwise comparisons. That random binary labels match structured occupancy (0.789 vs.\ 0.797, within $\sigma \approx 0.031$) confirms spatial content is secondary to gradient flow presence.

\textbf{Detach destabilizes optimization broadly.} Detach OCC exhibits TSR variance an order of magnitude above other conditions ($\sigma = 0.163$ vs.\ $< 0.050$): one seed collapses to TSR 0.624 with uniform degradation across all tasks, while its CAR (0.748) stays comparable to the other two seeds. Gradient detachment amplifies initialization sensitivity for action prediction without affecting collision behavior.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Overall results on SafeLIBERO Level~I (10 tasks). Bootstrap 95\% CIs via 10{,}000 hierarchical resamples. $^\dagger$Cutoff uses 2 seeds; all others use 3--4.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Condition & Seeds & TSR & CAR & $95\%$ CI & $\Delta$CAR \\
\midrule
Full OCC      & 3 & 0.894 $\pm$ 0.050 & \textbf{0.797} $\pm$ 0.032 & [.776, .817] & $+$1.7 \\
Random OCC    & 3 & 0.869 $\pm$ 0.029 & 0.789 $\pm$ 0.031 & [.767, .809] & $+$0.9 \\
Vanilla       & 3 & 0.907 $\pm$ 0.009 & 0.780 $\pm$ 0.012 & [.759, .801] & ---    \\
Detach OCC    & 3 & 0.812 $\pm$ 0.163 & 0.746 $\pm$ 0.002 & [.723, .768] & $-$3.4 \\
\midrule
Depth             & 4 & 0.889 $\pm$ 0.042 & 0.780 $\pm$ 0.039 & [.762, .797] & 0.0 \\
Cutoff$^\dagger$  & 2 & 0.915 $\pm$ 0.004 & 0.783 $\pm$ 0.021 & [.757, .808] & $+$0.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Factorial Decomposition}
\label{sec:mechanism}

Table~\ref{tab:factorial} arranges CAR in a $2{\times}2$ matrix crossing gradient flow with label content. The column effect (gradient flow) dominates: $+$5.1\,pp with structured labels (Full OCC vs.\ Detach), $+$0.9\,pp with random/no labels (Random OCC vs.\ Vanilla). The asymmetry arises because Detach runs the auxiliary head in the forward pass---BatchNorm statistics perturb the encoder without compensating auxiliary gradients---causing it to fall \emph{below} Vanilla rather than matching it (Section~\ref{sec:why_detach}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Factorial decomposition of CAR by gradient flow and label content. The column effect (gradient flow) dominates.}
\label{tab:factorial}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
 & Gradient ON & Gradient OFF \\
\midrule
Structured labels    & Full OCC (0.797) & Detach OCC (0.746) \\
Random / no labels   & Random OCC (0.789) & Vanilla (0.780) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Per-Task, Temporal, and Reference Frame Analysis}
\label{sec:additional}

\textbf{Per-task variation.} Seven of ten tasks achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla, leaving minimal room for improvement; measured effects concentrate on three physically constrained tasks (T3, T5, T6) where Vanilla CAR falls below 0.58. Detach is systematically worst on these tasks (T3 CAR 0.11, T5 CAR 0.05), but no consistent ordering emerges among gradient-flow conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:per_task}).

\textbf{Temporal extent (Cutoff$^\dagger$).} Cutoff achieves the highest TSR (0.915) but completely fails on the hardest safety task (T5 CAR 0.00), revealing distinct temporal requirements: task completion benefits durably from early-phase auxiliary training, while collision avoidance on physically constrained tasks requires continuous gradient flow.

\textbf{Egocentric depth.} Depth (4 seeds) matches Vanilla exactly (CAR 0.780) despite providing per-pixel geometric information directly aligned with collision avoidance. Three distinct label types---structured BEV occupancy, random binary maps, and egocentric depth---yield comparable CAR when gradient flow is maintained, reinforcing that gradient flow presence rather than spatial content is the operative factor.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

A factorial study on OpenVLA-OFT across six training conditions showed that auxiliary gradient flow through the vision encoder is necessary to prevent safety degradation when adding auxiliary heads. Conditions with gradient flow (Full OCC 0.797, Random OCC 0.789, Cutoff 0.783, Depth 0.780) maintained CAR comparable to Vanilla (0.780), whereas blocking gradients (Detach OCC 0.746) degraded CAR by 3.4\,pp with order-of-magnitude higher TSR variance. Three distinct label types achieve CAR within 1.7\,pp of each other, confirming that gradient flow presence---not spatial content---is the operative factor. These results yield a practical guideline (gradients must flow through the encoder when attaching auxiliary heads, with no ground-truth labels needed and zero inference cost) and a reusable factorial methodology for analyzing auxiliary task effects.

Open directions include testing additional auxiliary objectives (contrastive, reconstruction), gradient-level diagnostics to distinguish candidate mechanisms (Section~\ref{sec:why_gradient}), and evaluation on other architectures, dynamic obstacles, and real-world settings.

