\subsection{Per-Task Analysis}
\label{sec:per_task}

Seven of ten tasks achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla, leaving minimal room for improvement; measured effects concentrate on three physically constrained tasks (T3, T5, T6) where Vanilla CAR falls below 0.58. The overall CAR of 0.780 is dominated by these three hard tasks, which average 0.342 compared to 0.968 for the easy tasks (full breakdown in Appendix~\ref{app:per_task}).

\textbf{Detach is systematically worst on hard tasks.} Detach achieves the lowest CAR on T3 (0.11 vs.\ 0.31--0.38 for other conditions) and T5 (0.05 vs.\ 0.07--0.23), consistent with gradient detachment amplifying degradation where obstacle proximity is tight. Among gradient-flow conditions, no consistent ordering emerges: on T3, Vanilla (0.38) matches Full OCC (0.37); on T6, Full OCC (0.61) leads while Random OCC (0.55) trails. This reinforces the damage-prevention interpretation---gradient flow prevents Detach-specific degradation but does not systematically improve clearance.

\subsection{Temporal Dynamics and Reference Frame}
\label{sec:temporal}

\textbf{Cutoff$^\dagger$ reveals distinct temporal requirements for TSR and CAR.} Cutoff (gradient flow for 35K of 50K steps) achieves the highest TSR among all conditions (0.915) but loses collision avoidance on the hardest task (T5 CAR 0.00---the worst of any condition on any task). The CAR-preserving effect of gradient flow partially persists after removing auxiliary gradients (Cutoff CAR 0.783 vs.\ Detach 0.746, $+$3.7\,pp), but does not match continuous gradient flow (Full OCC 0.797, $+$1.4\,pp above Cutoff). Early-phase auxiliary gradients thus establish representations that partially resist degradation, but physically constrained tasks require continuous gradient flow throughout training.

\textbf{Egocentric depth matches Vanilla despite richer spatial content.} Depth (4 seeds) achieves CAR of 0.780 $\pm$ 0.039---identical to Vanilla (0.780 $\pm$ 0.012)---despite providing per-pixel geometric information directly aligned with collision avoidance. Three distinct label types (structured BEV occupancy, random binary maps, egocentric depth) yield comparable CAR when gradient flow is maintained, reinforcing that gradient flow presence is the operative factor. Depth exhibits the highest cross-seed CAR variance among gradient-flow conditions ($\sigma = 0.039$), with hard-task ranges of 0.20--0.78 (T3) and 0.16--0.84 (T6), consistent with auxiliary supervision amplifying seed sensitivity regardless of label content.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Why Does Gradient Flow Prevent Degradation?}
\label{sec:why_gradient}

The dominant pattern is not that gradient flow \emph{improves} CAR above Vanilla, but that its \emph{absence} degrades CAR below Vanilla. Three candidate mechanisms can explain this damage-prevention effect; all predict that gradient flow is necessary and label content is secondary.
(1)~\emph{Anti-overfitting regularization}: auxiliary gradients prevent the encoder from overfitting to action prediction, preserving feature diversity consistent with multi-task regularization theory~\cite{caruana1997multitask, lippl2024implicit}.
(2)~\emph{Optimization landscape smoothing}: the auxiliary loss modifies loss surface curvature, steering LoRA updates away from sharp minima that generalize poorly under distributional shift~\cite{foret2021sam}.
(3)~\emph{Gradient diversity}: auxiliary gradients provide an independent update direction that maintains the effective rank of weight updates, breaking correlations in the single-task gradient trajectory.
Distinguishing these hypotheses requires gradient-level diagnostics (cosine similarity, norm tracking, Hessian spectra); we leave this to future work.

\subsection{The Detach Degradation: BatchNorm Artifact or Gradient Flow Effect?}
\label{sec:why_detach}

Detach OCC falls 3.4\,pp below Vanilla despite identical architecture and forward computation to Full OCC. The auxiliary head's BatchNorm2d layers update running statistics at every forward pass regardless of gradient routing; with \texttt{stop\_gradient}, the encoder bears this statistical perturbation without compensating auxiliary gradients. This forward-pass interference makes Detach worse than Vanilla: the encoder absorbs the cost of the auxiliary pathway without receiving any training benefit. The TSR instability under Detach ($\sigma = 0.163$ vs.\ $< 0.050$ for all other conditions) confirms that gradient detachment destabilizes optimization broadly.

A discriminating test replaces BatchNorm2d with LayerNorm in the auxiliary head while keeping gradients detached. \textit{[Detach+LN results pending (plan\_027, 3 seeds); outcome determines whether the 3.4\,pp deficit is a normalization artifact or a genuine gradient-flow effect. See Section~\ref{app:extended} for the three scenario analyses.]}

\subsection{Implications and Limitations}
\label{sec:implications}

\textbf{Practical guideline.} If an auxiliary prediction head is attached to the vision encoder during VLA fine-tuning, gradients \emph{must} flow through the encoder. Blocking gradient flow degrades both collision avoidance ($-$3.4\,pp) and task execution stability. With gradient flow maintained, no ground-truth spatial labels are required (Random OCC matches Full OCC), and the auxiliary head is discarded after training at zero inference cost. This training-time intervention complements runtime safety methods such as CBF-based filtering~\cite{hu2025vlsa, raja2024ogmcbf}, which provide per-step guarantees but add inference cost.

\textbf{Limitations.} All experiments use a single architecture (OpenVLA-OFT, LoRA rank 32) on a single benchmark (SafeLIBERO Level~I, 10 tasks, static obstacles). The effect size among gradient-flow conditions is modest (0.0--1.7\,pp, within cross-seed variance); the robust finding is the Detach degradation ($-$3.4\,pp). All auxiliary conditions exhibit $2.6$--$3.1\times$ higher CAR variance than Vanilla, suggesting that auxiliary gradient flow introduces systematic sensitivity to initialization regardless of label content.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We investigated how auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning affects collision avoidance. A factorial study on OpenVLA-OFT across six training conditions revealed that auxiliary gradient flow through the vision encoder is necessary to prevent safety degradation when adding auxiliary heads, while spatial content plays a secondary role. Conditions with gradient flow---Full OCC (0.797), Random OCC (0.789), Cutoff (0.783), and Depth (0.780)---maintained CAR comparable to Vanilla (0.780), whereas Detach OCC fell 3.4\,pp below (0.746) with order-of-magnitude higher TSR variance. Three distinct label types achieve CAR within 1.7\,pp of each other, confirming that gradient flow presence---not spatial content---is the operative factor. The Cutoff condition reveals that early-phase gradient flow provides partial protection, but collision avoidance on the hardest tasks requires continuous auxiliary gradients throughout training.

These results yield a practical guideline---gradients must flow through the encoder when attaching auxiliary heads, with no ground-truth labels needed and zero inference cost---and a reusable factorial methodology for analyzing auxiliary task effects in VLA training.

Open directions include testing additional auxiliary objectives (contrastive, reconstruction), gradient-level diagnostics to distinguish the three candidate mechanisms in Section~\ref{sec:why_gradient}, and evaluation on other VLA architectures, dynamic obstacles, and real-world deployment settings.

