Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis} reveals a gradient collapse mechanism underlying the Detach degradation. Action loss gradient norms in the vision encoder are comparable across Vanilla and gradient-flow conditions (Full OCC and Random OCC produce 0.87--1.14$\times$ Vanilla norms), ruling out gradient amplification as the mechanism. Detach OCC, however, exhibits catastrophic gradient collapse: encoder gradient norms fall to 1--13\% of Vanilla across representative layers. With \texttt{stop\_gradient} blocking auxiliary gradients, the encoder receives near-zero gradient signal from the action loss alone---despite the action loss itself being unmodified.

This collapse pattern explains three key observations. First, it explains why Detach falls \emph{below} Vanilla: the encoder effectively stops learning, producing representations that degrade under continued LoRA updates to downstream layers. Second, it explains why label content is irrelevant: any auxiliary gradient flow (structured or random) maintains encoder gradient norms at Vanilla-comparable levels, preventing collapse regardless of signal content. Third, it explains the Detach TSR instability ($\sigma = 0.163$): seeds where the collapse is more severe suffer broader performance degradation.

Action and auxiliary gradients are approximately orthogonal throughout training (mean cosine similarity $\approx 0$, conflict rate $\approx 50\%$), indicating that the auxiliary task provides an independent gradient direction rather than reinforcing or opposing the action gradient. This orthogonality is consistent with the collapse prevention interpretation: the auxiliary gradient maintains encoder updates along a complementary subspace, preventing the effective rank of encoder gradients from collapsing.

